The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says the 412 reports are one issue with one fix queued.
Its own cause table, built from the same export, already disagrees. Read every report by
its tenant and its body, and #4471 holds three problems. Each now has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix will not close #4471. Two of its problems have a different cause and a different fix.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} repo="https://github.com/mwakulinski/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows" />

## The three problems

1. **The shared parser reads dates month first.** Every tenant on file writes the day first, so
   their dates come out swapped: "Our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July." This is the bug the
   #4471 page describes, and the queued fix.
2. **The booking-v3 form hint asks for `MM/DD/YYYY`.** People type what the hint shows, month
   first, and the form reads it the tenant's way. Only form reports from the three v3 tenants.
   The fix is the hint, not the parser.
3. **Invites move by one day.** No swap: "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday
   11 May." · "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting." The desk
   already marked these as a one-day move in `desk-notes.json`, then filed them under the parser
   by tenant. A day/month fix cannot move a date by one day.

## Why they were merged

Triage merges on subject ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). The 412
reports use twelve subjects, and the problems share them: "Date is wrong" and "Wrong day on the
invite" each carry reports of all three. Subject matching cannot tell them apart. Intake's tag does not help either: it guesses from the
reporter's browser, and it disagrees with the tenant's cause on many reports (column "intake tag wrong").

## The desk's own view, for comparison

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Lessons

- A count is not a cause. Read the reports by tenant and body before marking a big ticket known.
- Matching on subject merges different bugs that people describe in the same words.

